A||||30||Cozmo||Looking for a good inexpensive digital camera||||5||
Z||000000||Cozmo||05-21-2001||10:47 AM||||I'm getting ready to make the leap from a film style to a digital camera;  Getting too lazy to shoot, develop, scan etc. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>What kinds have some of you got, and what would you recommend?<BR>C02M0||||reg||5
Z||000001||JSocha||05-21-2001||11:03 AM||||Stick with your film camera!<P>IMO, its not worth the hassle to go Digital.  I have a Sony Mavica FD-7 and we recently bought our sales department a new Professional Digital with the highest resolution on the market for about $3K just because they wanted it for the road to take pictures for literature (even after I argued that we should put the money into a film camera).  Sales is always asking me to bring my Nikon N70 35mm SLR to work because they can not get the quality they want in a picture from the Digital and wished they would have listened.<P>In the end, you get better clarity with the film then you ever will with a Digital and your scanner will always pick up more colors off the photo then what the digital camera can.<P>35mm SLR camera's are in most cases alot cheaper then what you will pay for a top of the line digital.  Plus, you don't have the hassle of memory cards and other electronic problems.  Stay away from the Olympus 3030 if you decide to over look this advise.  We ordered two of them and they both went back with problems before we bought the company's first Nikon digital and then later on the other as engineering took the first one to use to take 'quick' pictures of prototypes.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||05-21-2001||02:06 PM||||Digital photography is OK if A&gt; you aren't expecting glossy magazine quality images and B&gt; you aren't gadget challenged. I have two Sony cameras, a DSC-F505 still camera and a DCR-TRV20 camcorder. There have been improvements to the still camera since I got mine (almost 2 yrs ago), but it does fine for web graphics and smaller prints. Since it only goes up to 1280 X 1024 the quality of a print falls off over a 5 X 7. I have had film prints made from my 640 X 480 shots and they are just fine. It plugs into a USB port and appears as an extra hard drive in Windows. Drag & drop to import photos then I use Paint Shop to dress them up. The little pic in my sig below was taken with it.<P>I decided to invest in the camcorder when I started autocrossing. I can use the Firewire cable to import digital video into my Windows ME movie editor. It's kind of tedious but fun to be able to share stuff with. My <A HREF="http://fearlessdata.com/fiero/0429_87GT_AX.wmv" TARGET=_blank>autocross video</A> is about 5.7MB and a kick to be able to share it with everyone.<P>It takes a while to get enough practice to really use digital cameras and they are definitely not for you if you have trouble setting your VCR clock or programming favorite channels on your TV. Even with a scanner, you need to get good at editing the images once you have imported them onto your computer.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fearlessdata.com/fiero/87GT_small.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||thomas_l||05-21-2001||03:23 PM||||Oh, you said <I>inexpensive</I>. Sorry man! As far as I know, you'd be hard pressed to get great results from any sub-$300 camera. The low-end models do OK for web graphics but not great for printing or large images. For the money, a great 35mm SLR will beat a digital camera at the expense of instant gratification  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-21-2001||04:38 PM||||Try one of those new 'Pen Cams'. I tried one there about the size of a large fountain pen. There pic quality is pretty decent and they also double as a live Web-Cam. Some hold near 100 pics on a memory stick. They also make 20 second videos. They range from 20 pics to 100 and pricewise from $79-$150. I have some lit I could email you if you want.||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||05-21-2001||05:51 PM||||If you're talking cheap, I have a Polaroid Fun Flash 640 which takes pretty decent pictures, has an LCD viewfinder, and was less than $80 at Wal-Mart.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/may08$01_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||05-22-2001||08:32 AM||||I'm pretty happy with my Polaroid PDC-700.  I paid $220 at Wal-mart about 18 months ago; I don't know if you call that "inexpensive".<P>These photos were taken with it: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007123.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007123.html</A> <BR>(Photos have been cropped and scaled using PhotoMax Pro software, which came with the camera.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cozmo||05-22-2001||10:31 AM||||   I guess I should have been more specific...<BR>   I've got some pretty nice film stuff (Mamyia-Sekor 500DTL with a slew of lenses, etc) but for "point and shoot" grandma vacation pictures it sucks.<BR>   I want something butt simple for when my purposes are not "fine photography"; but documentation and web shots.  I can live with 640x480 resolution and no LCD display.<BR>Prefer under $150...<BR>Thanks,<BR><B>C02M0</B><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||01:27 PM||||I got one and now I cant remember who its made by - its similar to the polaroid one, with no lcd on the back<P>  I want to say mitsubishi?! motorola?! It was less than $90.<P>  the only shortcoming is it has a wide angle lens like most snapshot cameras, it prefer about 100mm lens on a 35mm format camera for almost everything (2x), but other than that, its very handly to have lying around to snap off some quick shots, and you can post them on the web in minutes.<P>  there are a couple of 640 cameras like this around for less than $90. <P>  i wouldnt bother with a 320 camera. I would like to find a 640 digital camera with a 2x OPTICAL (thank you) zoom for $120 or so.<P>  prices on digital cameras are dropping like crazy. everyone should have one in thier glove compartment all the time - looks like watch cameras will be getting reasonable soon too.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||05-22-2001||08:51 PM||||Keep a watch..... several makers including HP Kodak Polaroid and others are slated to drop megapixel+ cammeras on the market for $200-300. Some are already out. <P>These basic cameras should do well for allot of stuff that will only end up as GIF or JPEG on the web anyway.<P>Competition in this market is heating up, you'll see some nice zoom jobs drop in price quickly as well.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick's Dad||05-22-2001||09:21 PM||||Another film camera plug:<P>If all you want is quick and easy, then a good 35mm point and shoot will do it. The Olympus Stylus Epic will do what you want (take great shots quickly) in your price range ($80-90 standard, about $110 Deluxe, with panorama, case and battery). Photo Techniques, a pretty hoity toity mag picked it amont the top 25 cameras, joined by Leicas, Mamiyas (645's not 35mm), Sinars, and more. It's smaller than pocket sized, and weather resistant.<P>Film, thus far, registers tons more detail than digital. You can always get your film scanned to CD-ROM by a reputable dealer, and they'll be printable. 640x480 doesn't even make a good 3x5.||||reg||1
Z||000011||MrPBody||05-22-2001||09:22 PM||||Cozmo --<P>Glad to hear about another old Mamiya-Sekor alive and well.  I have a 1000DTL (love those mechanical noises!) and the world's cheapest telephoto: a K-mart "Focal" preset 200 mm with doubler.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Eric_PA||05-22-2001||09:56 PM||||Keep an eye out at Wallmarts. I got a Polaroid PDC640 for $120. It has a LCD display, 640x480 resolution, and 8MB of memory on a removable card. Sometimes this camera goes on sale for $99. Not bad for documenting and web photos. I am happy with it.<BR>Here is a sample pic from the recent Carlisle show:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/May19$18.jpg"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||MrPBody||05-22-2001||09:59 PM||||Sorry about sequential posts.  (Bad timing.)<P>I agree that film captures better detail than any digital camera I can afford; however, day-to-day snapshots don't use all that detail.  In practical use my 1024x768 Polaroid produces decent 3"x5" prints, and even 5"x7" look OK.  (HP printer.)  For me, the virtues of digital are:<P><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>I don't have to shoot a whole roll before processing<BR>-<BR><LI>I can print only the photos I want<BR>-<BR><LI>I don't have to deal with crappy automated processing<BR>-<BR><LI>It's way cheaper than scanning to CD (~$10/roll)<BR></UL><P>It does take some work to set-up and print digital photos; the camera eats batteries, doesn't have a zoom, and it washes-out bright highlights; and it's easy to lose images to computer mishaps unless you burn them to CD.<P>But since I got a digital, I seldom shoot film.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||10:44 PM||||as much as I love my 35mm cameras and all my lenses and the touch and feel and technology of them, the handwriting is on the wall for film.<P>  digital cameras will do to film what camcorders did to 8mm movie cameras. At first camcorders were hugh and expensive, and now, well try to go buy a new 8mm movie camera somewhere, or to even buy a roll of film for one.<P>  Its just a matter of time. Film is dead, it just doesnt know it yet, like when the coyote walks off the edge of the cliff, but hasnt looked down yet.||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroSTETZ||05-23-2001||03:35 AM||||I work at CompUSA, and right now theres a logitech camera that takes 640x480 photos, doubles as a webcam, and captures video at 30fps WITH SOUND its 149.99 and theres a rebate to make it 99.99 - got my girlfriend's dad one for his birthday (employee cost is a little less, barely worth it- would think they'd be nicer to their techs)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.shawnrocks.com/SHAWN.GIF"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||JSocha||05-23-2001||11:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>Its just a matter of time. Film is dead, it just doesnt know it yet, like when the coyote walks off the edge of the cliff, but hasnt looked down yet.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree with you completely.  Film has had many more years of advances in its developement over that of the digital cameras and will continue to do so in the future.<P>Nothing now or in the future with Digital cameras will be able to beat the crispness, color spectrum, clarity and detail that can be captured on film.  Even if it could, the processing of such digital images would be limited by the printer or development system thereafter used.<P>For instance, our company recently bought a Fujustfilm FinePix S1 Pro, 6.1 Megapixel Digital SLR camera with a resolution of 3040 x 2016 just for our sales department, just because they felt digital was the way to go.<P>Sales intention was to use it for literature (since our Engineering department claimed the Nikon Coolpix 990 digital we originally bought for sales to take pictures of prototypes that come in) when they were on the road.  To this day, when it comes to photos they want for their literature, they ask me to bring in my Nikon N70, 35mm "film" camera to use to take the pictures that the digital camera is unable to take or to borrow it on the road.  An expensive $3k paperweight is what this digital camera has become.<P>I'll take any el-cheapo, 35mm, Rollaflex or Mamiya "film" camera over any digital any day, now or in the future.<P>I would also be willing to challenge you to the quality of a negative to that of a digital image when it comes to the actual print; exploded to any size (3x5 to poster size and beyond) taken at any distance of lets say a sailboat's spinnaker sail (the colorful balloon sail) and show you the color, sharpness, clarity, crispness and every last crease of that sail as it happened at the time to which no digital camera would ever be able to reproduce in a million years without dithering or blurring as it gets larger.  I'd probably <P>The "negative" image from film is far more superior and film will always continue to advance in its ability to capture and reproduce what it takes now and into the future.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||11:19 AM||||I agree with you 100% - film produces a better quality image all the way around.<P>that doesnt matter - 8mm Kodachrome movie film images are way superior to any video camcorder / TV monitor system you can buy today - but 8mm film is still dead.<P>Hollywood kept using film long after camcorders replaced 8mm, and 16mm held on for a long time for amature film makers. But even hollywood it going digital video now. Can they beat a 70mm movie image on video - no! does it matter? No!<P>  Film will get better only marginally over the years, its a very mature product already. Digital video and still cameras are just starting out, and they will improve vastly over the next several years.<P>   forget the printers! when you have photos printed what do you do with them? You look at them for 10 seconds then put them in a shoe box for the next 5 years, when you will look at them for 10 seconds again...<P>  digital images can be seen right now, no developing. You can view them full screen on you PC or laptop, so you dont need to print or enlarge them, they are all 15" or 17" or 21" diagonal, whatever size monitor you have.<P>   I like film, it is better quality. But its still dead. [If you listen carefully you can hear taps playing softly in the distance - behind the Kodak factory].||||reg||1
Z||000018||JSocha||05-23-2001||12:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>when you have photos printed what do you do with them? You look at them for 10 seconds then put them in a shoe box for the next 5 years, when you will look at them for 10 seconds again...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmmmm.....How true that seems to be!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Alex4mula||05-23-2001||01:16 PM||||High volume sales of film equipment will die to digital. It's a matter of time (10-20yrs?). Still you will find some for a niche market. Look at turtable vynil records players. You still can find them.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||01:48 PM||||the fact that digital photos are FREE is another nail in the coffin for film. If you shoot a roll of film you might get half the photos to come out well enough and interesting enough to keep<P> and a roll of film and developing is about $15 to $20 depending on its speed and size...<P>but a digial camera is Free Freee Freeee after you buy it - if you have a laptop with you - all the better - you can upload the photos as you take them and reset the camera memory - delete the ones you dont like - reshoot the ones that didnt come out right there on the spot.<P>  when it comes to taking photos, for 99% of the market quality is not the most important thing, cost and convienance are.<P>  when I can get a 1024 x 768 camera with a 2x optical zoom for $150 to maybe $200, that can hold 15 pictures or more - my 35mm SLRs are gonna start collecting alot of dust.<P>  what some genius needs to do is make a digital camera that accepts 35mm lenses, so we can use our great zooms and our 500mm lenses from our SLRs (for a low cost)||||reg||1
Z||000021||ferg||05-23-2001||02:32 PM||||They already have digital cameras that accept 35mm lenses. I get this catalog that deals with publishing stuff- (cd burning, clip art, re-writeable dvd's) and in this catalog they have a digital camera body that uses 35mm lenses- it's not cheap either (around $3500. It looks like it's mainly for studio photographers, but it's out there.<P>As for film becoming obsolete- I think for most consumers, with a computer it is. I haven't bought a roll of film in several years- the digital is too convenient for my purposes. I have a Sony DSC-s30 digital camera- this thing is great- it has the memory sticks which are awesome- it came w/ a 4mb stick- on the 640x480 setting, I can take about 40 pictures, I also have an 8 mb stick as a back-up. The quality is sooo much better than any film camera I've owned. I think most people don't use/need a 35mm camera body- most like the point and click. However, I think film will still be with us for a long time to come. Some things just can't be replaced that easily. Take Ansel Adams- his work is incredible- I don't see that type of photography going away anytime soon, but that type of photography is a niche market.<P>Let's face it, most people even if they have a great 35mm set-up, don't know how to use it to the fullest extent- it's an ego thing. Plus lugging all that stuff around is a real pain- ever see professional photographers? They have about 3 camera bags around their neck, who wants that?<P>The digital vs. film debate will mainly come down to the use. Photographers doing portraits, or artists, etc- will stick with film. People who have decent computer skills, and who mainly take quick pictures will like the digital better.<p>[This message has been edited by ferg (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||JSocha||05-23-2001||02:59 PM||||Apparently, some people have not gone to a RITZ Camera chain store or other photo development store.<P>Back in '98 when we bought our Sony Mavica Digital camera, we took it on our honeymoon.<P>Took over 600 photos (over 28 diskettes) and tried to print them on our "PHOTO" quality Lexmark Printer we bought.  Looked like $hit.<P>So, we took the diskettes down to our local RITZ Camera store to have them print them out.  Cost:  "$11.00 per file!"<P>Went to a couple of other photo stores since and the cost ranges from $9.00 to $15.00 per file to have them printed out.<P>I do have the photos here at work and at home on our computer, but it would be nice to share some of our photos of our Carribian cruise that we took on Holland America with family members.<P>You can talk about FREE all you wish.  It just depends on what part of the boat you wish or need to be on I guess.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||04:49 PM||||Kodak did this thing for a while a few years back, you sent a roll of film to them for processing and they printed them out AND put the photos on a CD for you.<P>now they have a program where you email your jpgs to them and they print them out and send them to you in the mail.<P>Talk about living in the past and being stuck in the box - they just dont get it.<P>  If you can see them on your monitor, or on your TV screen, you dont need them printed out - unless you want to mail them to grandma who doesnt have a PC.<P> the next big thing will be palm pilots with color 640 displays, or with a little thing you attach to your glasses that lets you see a 1280 x 1024 color image with no LCD.<P>  then you will see a big dive in printers, copiers and faxmachines too.||||reg||1
Z||000024||thomas_l||05-24-2001||02:47 PM||||The only thing separating the digital from film cameras is the cost of data storage (both dollars and media size) and flexibility. If you had an infinitely large storage media and infinite bandwidth to publish or e-mail all your infinitely HUGE image files, you could better film quality very easily.<P>HOWEVER, it all boils down to how many bits of data define each pixel (color depth), pixel density (image quality), and compression efficiency and accuracy (file size, data loss). So here and now, it's just not practical to match film quality. If a picture is over 100KB it's too big and slow for people using modems. Maybe years from now when color depth is measured in Billions of colors and Gigabyte files are no big deal for the average home user -  that will be the death of film.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||03:57 PM||||well lets see now.<P>the cheapo 640 by 480 camera I got for my daughter was less than $100 (Its an RCA -checked last night)<P>it holds 15 photos at a time and they are about 25k bytes in size each for normal jpeg compression.<P> a CD rom holds 640 Million bytes of data - thats 25,600 photos.<P> and a blank CDR cost 20 cents now<P> compaired to a 24 exposure roll of film, cost 3 or 4 bucks, cost 10 or $12 to develope.<P>  so thats $0.000781 per photo to take and store digital photos on a cdr<P>  and $0.58 per photo to take 35mm photos, have then developed and store them in a shoe box<P>   or 747 times as much to use film.<P>  if you use film you still have to mail the photos to grandma, you can mail 1 cd with 25,600 photos on it for the same 35 cent stamp.<P>   Film is already dead - it just hasnt hit the floor yet.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||05:05 PM||||that is also part of the economics that killed the 8mm movie camera. A roll of 8mm film cost you $10 or so including developing, and they were 3.5 minutes long.<P> a tape for a standard camcorder is 2 hours long and costs about a buck!<P>  so the 8mm movie film was $340 for 2 hours<P>  and the camcorder is $1 for 2 hours<P>  340 times as expensive for the film.<P>  quality?! whats that?||||reg||1
Z||000027||JSocha||05-24-2001||11:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>if you use film you still have to mail the photos to grandma, you can mail 1 cd with 25,600 photos on it for the same 35 cent stamp.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmmm?  But wouldn't it get expensive again as now you would have to buy "grandma" a computer and also pay for classes so she can use the computer and view the now sent $0.55 CD with the 25,600 photos on it?<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000028||Cozmo||05-25-2001||07:58 AM||||No question that film will rule for a long time for hi-res applications.  Digital will eventually catch up but it suffers from "technological obsolescence" I.E. this years $500 camera in 2 years will be outperformed by a $150 camera.  You pay to be on the "bleeding edge".<BR>Mrpbody, you're right! I love all the cool noises my Mamyia makes... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><BR>Been doing some research, the prices are really dropping!  Two that have piqued my interest are:<BR>Kodak DC3200<BR>HP photosmart 215<BR>Both are priced right ans do what I want.  Anyone have any experience with either?<BR>Thanks,<BR><B>C02M0</B> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||10:33 AM||||Saw one at BJ's that looks tempting<P>Panasonic 1280 x 960 resolution, 3X OPTICAL zoom, has LCD on back - $240<P>very temped - maybe when it gets ot $200 I'll grab one.<P>something I found that also works good - I have a video capture card in my PC and can connect my Hi8 camcorder to it and grab frames.<P>  you only get 640 x 480 resolution, but my camcorder has a 10X optical zoom, and you can get a lot of images on a 2 hour tape, and capture them later on the PC as photos.<P>   takes super close ups too, focuses down to like 1/2" from the lens to infinity.||||reg||1
Z||000030||rogergarrison||05-29-2001||04:57 PM||||THis same type of argument went on when VHS video recorders wiped out BETA. VHS overwhelmed the market with a then very inferior product. Beta still will rival any VHS in picture quality, but due to VHS marketing, they went the way of the dinosaur. Yes film is by far the best for quality now, but its very expensive with buying film, developing, etc. Digital is going to overtake it at some point. Its just too simple and convienent with everyone having home computers now. Now you can send a pic of the new baby to grandparents 10,000 miles away within seconds of being born. cant do that with film. Not too mention for example on vacation, taking some great pictures and get home to find out the camera had a lite leak, or film was bad. what do you do , go back and retake them. with digital, you can take it and make sure you got what you want before you even leave.I can take 500 pics on a $6 pack of disks, and if I use the superdisk (1500 per disk) on the same pack of disks I can take 45,000 pics. try that with film.||||reg||1
